/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da95c20f-dc58-5edc-8dcb-dd5058ac04ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472968} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7455f8-3833-5279-8d9e-7da8601cff21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab7455f8-3833-5279-8d9e-7da8601cff21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472969} :answer-field/id #uuid "f58adf13-8680-5744-bc7c-9ac757d84107" :answer-field/key "$X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213599" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbc5f5af-b932-595b-9f45-d7822f805699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472928} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d188d8-7801-5c0a-9e1e-a2aa6485adf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0d188d8-7801-5c0a-9e1e-a2aa6485adf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472929} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b1ff480-810f-507d-a2d1-a039d0e78424" :answer-field/key "![Digraph I](images/e9/e98b734b146f83379a7511ab9ee9ac92814f71c42843bdb0a91541f45f4c965b.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473859} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32592e22-95ce-53db-acfa-46e6ac2385e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472930} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca0a8a2-f51f-5ad7-800d-598564f182ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32592e22-95ce-53db-acfa-46e6ac2385e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472930} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeaf136c-cb01-591e-bfe6-c7fd00a377a8" :answer-field/key "![Digraph II](images/21/21e5e2264f0483b3fffeb05e845e6c7994c8dc90c81045c272bd29d0a9378ce6.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473860} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5a592fe-3124-53ec-b419-b1008565d345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472932} {:answer/id #uuid "15a5c949-7bc0-58e4-a982-02954107b625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5a592fe-3124-53ec-b419-b1008565d345" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472932} :answer-field/id #uuid "2cbec2c6-432a-5be0-b414-344192c413ec" :answer-field/key "![Digraph III](images/71/713c0e5a8dcbc8b420ddc02fdce873fd9554ae3c7004be1be48400475f6f9ff2.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213318" :question/problem "Which of the below digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is digraph I only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b72c5fe-7cf1-5d4c-8244-863dd48fbd1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472946} {:answer/id #uuid "a56406c4-8dd3-577e-9eeb-8b1dd58a4181" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b72c5fe-7cf1-5d4c-8244-863dd48fbd1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472946} :answer-field/id #uuid "10e53a65-986c-5958-99b5-5f4a013df2f8" :answer-field/key "![Plot I](images/81/812ffc05c4714a19d0a12d09f0233b994b97a369adb6ae33d3c31bee0e7a4980.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adc2b2c3-99e6-5873-938c-abe360fed0ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472948} {:answer/id #uuid "6d05c6a6-6c94-5079-9dc8-1d06f4cfa059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d05c6a6-6c94-5079-9dc8-1d06f4cfa059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472949} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f04fe0d-c9ba-5fb1-b2a5-dee9ef3c2597" :answer-field/key "![Plot II](images/b7/b705e631f67e4c4d26846771708cd3d5e8660d032f2cfa95ccac4d1695400e85.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473869} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dbc29c4-0f7d-531f-babc-73e9e8ec5022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472950} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c8355b-ea42-5d1a-9524-335addcefe34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dbc29c4-0f7d-531f-babc-73e9e8ec5022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472950} :answer-field/id #uuid "60efff53-6597-5c41-9085-3dc22abcbc74" :answer-field/key "![Plot III](images/e2/e2747fa47e91037fb0812fbc243675a088e9ee668190b9ac738a123c3129409a.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213492" :question/problem "Which of the plots below represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is plot II only"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/